---
/**
 * The opening screen.
 *
 * A blank field with the name writing itself, which then lifts to reveal the
 * page. Shown once per visit, on the homepage.
 *
 * A screen that covers the site is the riskiest thing on it, so three
 * properties matter more than the animation does:
 *
 * 1. **It cannot be stranded.** It is dismissed by a CSS animation that ends
 *    hidden, not by JavaScript. Script only ever starts it. If script dies
 *    immediately afterwards, the screen still lifts on time.
 * 2. **It never waits for anything.** No load event, no asset, no network. A
 *    fixed length, decided up front. The usual failure here is a screen that
 *    waits for something that never arrives and leaves the site blank while
 *    every request returned perfectly normally.
 * 3. **It cannot block the page.** It ignores the pointer throughout, so the
 *    content underneath stays clickable even while it is on screen.
 *
 * It is absent entirely for anyone who has asked for less motion, anyone who
 * has already seen it this visit, and anyone without JavaScript — the markup
 * stays inert until a script in the document head opts into it.
 */
import mark from "@/assets/wordmark/mark.svg?raw"

/* Its own mask id, so it cannot collide with the lettering elsewhere. */
const svg = mark.replaceAll("wordmark-wipe", "intro-wipe")
---

<div class="intro" aria-hidden="true">
  <div class="intro__mark" set:html={svg} />
</div>

<style is:global>
  /*
   * Global for the same reason as the lettering's own styles: the markup below
   * is injected, so it never receives Astro's scoping attribute and a scoped
   * rule would match nothing. Namespaced under `.intro` instead.
   *
   * The timing, in one place:
   *
   *   writing   180ms wait, then 700ms per letter, 80ms apart   → 1600ms
   *   hold      450ms                                           → 2050ms
   *   slide     750ms                                           → 2800ms
   *
   * The hero lettering starts rising at 2050ms, so the reveal and the arrival
   * overlap instead of queueing.
   *
   * Deliberately unhurried. It is the one moment on the site that exists to be
   * watched rather than read, and at the earlier speed it was over before the
   * eye had followed the word across. Everything that makes it safe — it never
   * waits on anything, it cannot be stranded, it never takes the pointer —
   * holds at any length, so the length is free to be a taste decision.
   */
  .intro {
    display: none;
  }

  :root[data-intro] .intro {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 100;
    background-color: var(--color-paper);
    /* Never intercepts a click, even mid-fade. */
    pointer-events: none;
    animation: intro-lift 2800ms linear forwards;
  }

  :root[data-intro] .intro__mark {
    width: min(64vw, 900px);
  }

  .intro__mark svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  .intro__mark .mark__ghost path {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 12%, transparent);
  }

  .intro__mark .mark__draw path {
    fill: currentColor;
  }

  /*
   * What animates is a mask, never the letter. Stroking a letter's outline and
   * animating the dash offset traces its perimeter — up one side and back down
   * the other — and draws the enclosed shapes in a, u and o as separate loops.
   * Rendered, that gives hollow colouring-book letters rather than writing.
   * Uncovering the real filled letter avoids it, and the finished state is
   * identical to static lettering.
   *
   * The pale copy underneath is structural rather than decoration: it holds
   * the final width from the first frame, so the word does not jump as the
   * letters land.
   */
  :root[data-intro] .intro__mark mask rect {
    transform-box: fill-box;
    transform-origin: left center;
    transform: scaleX(0);
    animation: mark-write 700ms var(--ease-draw) forwards;
    animation-delay: calc(var(--i) * 80ms + 180ms);
  }

  @keyframes mark-write {
    to {
      transform: scaleX(1);
    }
  }

  /*
   * Holds, then slides up out of the way. Ending hidden is what makes this
   * self-dismissing.
   *
   * It slides rather than fades. Fading crossfades two different compositions
   * — the centred word on this screen and the large cropped one in the hero —
   * and for a few hundred milliseconds you see both at once, at different
   * sizes, overlapping. Sliding reveals the page underneath cleanly instead,
   * with nothing doubled.
   */
  @keyframes intro-lift {
    0% {
      transform: translateY(0);
      visibility: visible;
      animation-timing-function: linear;
    }
    73.2% {
      transform: translateY(0);
      animation-timing-function: cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1);
    }
    100% {
      transform: translateY(-100%);
      visibility: hidden;
    }
  }

  /* The hero lettering waits for the reveal, but only when there is one. */
  :root[data-intro] .intro-follows {
    --hero-enter-delay: 2050ms;
  }
</style>
